  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nIsolated light showers may be ongoing Thursday morning across far\nNE OK and NW AR, but with minimal impacts. A few isolated showers\nand thunderstorms will be possible again during the afternoon \nhours, with highest potential again occurring generally along and \nnorth of Hwy 412. By late Thursday evening and overnight, higher \nprecipitation potential will develop along the frontal zone in NE \nOK and far NW AR as a stronger shortwave moves into the region \nwith a strengthening LLJ in the lower levels. This shower/storm \npotential will likely persist into Friday and some redevelopment \nwill be possible during the afternoon hours. Severe weather is not\nexpected, but any storms in the area will be capable of producing\nheavy rainfall and some strong wind gusts. Given dry antecedent \nconditions, flooding concerns will remain very low. Rain chances \ndecrease for this weekend and into early next week as upper level \nridging builds back over our area.\n\nApart from local deviations caused by precipitation or prolonged \ncloud cover, high temperatures will generally remain steady in the\nmid 90s to lower 100s over the next several days. Dewpoints will \nnot be as high as during our last heatwave, but certainly still \nhigh enough to produce dangerous heat indices through at least \nearly next week. Thus, additional heat headlines appear probable, \nthough the exact configuration Thursday and Friday will depend on \nconvective trends in NE OK and NW AR. For now, have issued a Heat \nAdvisory Thursday for much of the same areas as Wednesday, namely \nthe Tulsa metro and AR River Valley. With the expectation for more\ncloud cover near the OK-KS border, the advisory does not cover \nthese areas at this time. However, local expansions may be \nrequired in future forecast updates based on observational \ntrends. Aside from the heat, low impact weather is expected \nthrough the remainder of the forecast period.",
